= 1B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= Solution
{parent=1B}

The differential operator is $(D-3)^2$, where $D=d/dx$. Factoring out the forcing's exponential removes this repeated <characteristic root of a constant-coefficient differential equation>: set $y=e^{3x}v$. Differentiation gives $(D-3)y=e^{3x}v'$ and $(D-3)^2y=e^{3x}v''$, so $v''=\cos(2x)$. Two integrations yield
$$
v=-\frac14\cos(2x)+Ax+B.
$$
The <initial conditions> become $v(0)=0$ and $v'(0)+3v(0)=1$, hence $B=1/4$ and $A=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=e^{3x}\left[x+\frac{1-\cos(2x)}4\right].}
$$
The factorization verifies the <differential equation> directly, and the displayed expression has the required value and derivative at zero.

= 2B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= i
{parent=2b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $F(y)=(y^2-4)\arctan y$. Its zeros, and hence the <equilibrium points>, are $-2,0,2$. For a perturbation $y=y_*+\eta$, <linear stability analysis> gives $\dot\eta=F'(y_*)\eta+O(\eta^2)$, with
$$
F'(y)=2y\arctan y+\frac{y^2-4}{1+y^2}.
$$
At zero, $F'(0)=-4$, so sufficiently small perturbations decay and **$y=0$ is asymptotically stable**. At either outer <equilibrium point>, $F'(\pm2)=4\arctan2>0$, so **$y=-2$ and $y=2$ are unstable**. The <phase line> confirms the result: $F$ has signs $-,+,-,+$ on the intervals cut out by $-2,0,2$, respectively. Flow points toward zero on both sides and away from both outer equilibria.

= ii
{parent=2b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The constant solutions are <fixed points> of $G(u)=u^2(1+u)/2$. Solving $G(u)=u$ gives $u(u+2)(u-1)=0$, hence $u_*=0,-2,1$. For a <discrete dynamical system>, a small perturbation satisfies $\eta_{n+1}=G'(u_*)\eta_n+O(\eta_n^2)$, where
$$
G'(u)=u+\frac32u^2,\qquad G'(0)=0,\quad G'(-2)=4,\quad G'(1)=\frac52.
$$
A multiplier of magnitude less than one contracts perturbations; a multiplier greater than one expands them. Therefore **$u=0$ is asymptotically stable, while $u=-2$ and $u=1$ are unstable**. In particular, if $|u|\leq1/2$, then $|G(u)|\leq(3/4)|u|^2\leq(3/8)|u|$, proving convergence to zero without relying solely on the vanishing linear multiplier.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= Solution
{parent=3F}

For $0\leq u\leq1$, <independence> gives
$$
P(U>u)=P(X>u,Y>u)=(1-u)^2.
$$
Thus $U$ has <probability density function> $f_U(u)=2(1-u)$, and its <expected value> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EU=\int_0^1 2u(1-u)\,du=\frac13.}
$$
Pointwise, $U+V=X+Y$ and $UV=XY$. By linearity of <expectation>, $\mathbb EV=1-1/3=2/3$; by <independence>, $\mathbb E(UV)=\mathbb EX\,\mathbb EY=1/4$. Consequently the <covariance of two uniform order statistics> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(U,V)=\mathbb E(UV)-\mathbb EU\,\mathbb EV
=\frac14-\frac29=\frac1{36}.}
$$
The <order statistics> are dependent despite the independence of the original observations.

= 4F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= Solution
{parent=4F}

The <moment-generating function> of a <random variable> $X$ is $M_X(t)=\mathbb E(e^{tX})$, defined for real $t$ where that expectation is finite. For a <standard normal distribution>, completing the square gives
$$
M_X(t)=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{tx-x^2/2}\,dx
=e^{t^2/2}\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-(x-t)^2/2}\,dx
=\boxed{e^{t^2/2}}.
$$
It is finite for every real $t$. Derivatives at zero give the <moments>: the expansion $e^{t^2/2}=1+t^2/2+t^4/8+O(t^6)$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EX^0=1,\quad \mathbb EX=0,\quad \mathbb EX^2=1,
\quad \mathbb EX^3=0,\quad \mathbb EX^4=3.}
$$
For $Y$ with the <normal distribution> of mean $\mu$ and variance $\sigma^2$, write $Y=\mu+\sigma X$ in distribution. Its <moment-generating function> is consequently
$$
\boxed{M_Y(t)=e^{\mu t}M_X(\sigma t)
=\exp(\mu t+\sigma^2t^2/2).}
$$
This formula also covers the constant random variable when $\sigma=0$.

= 5B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= i
{parent=5b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In the normalized second-order <linear differential equation>, an <ordinary point> $z_0$ is one at which both $p$ and $q$ are <holomorphic functions> in a neighbourhood of $z_0$. A <singular point of a differential equation> is a point that is not ordinary. A singular point $z_0$ is a <regular singular point> if $(z-z_0)p(z)$ and $(z-z_0)^2q(z)$ extend analytically to $z_0$. Equivalently, $p$ has at most a simple pole and $q$ at most a double pole there. If a singular point fails this test, it is an <irregular singular point>. The normalized form matters: zeros of a leading coefficient must first be assessed after division by that coefficient.

= ii
{parent=5b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $z\ne0$, division by $4z$ gives $p(z)=1/(2z)$ and $q(z)=1/(4z)$. Thus zero is singular, but $zp=1/2$ and $z^2q=z/4$ are analytic. **Zero is a regular singular point.**

Apply the <Frobenius method> with $y=z^r\sum_{n=0}^\infty a_nz^n$, $a_0\ne0$. The lowest power $z^{r-1}$ has coefficient $[4r(r-1)+2r]a_0$, so the <indicial equation> is $2r(2r-1)=0$. Its <characteristic exponents at a regular singular point> are $0$ and $1/2$. At the next powers,
$$
2(n+r)(2(n+r)-1)a_n+a_{n-1}=0,\qquad n\geq1.
$$
With $a_0=1$, the two <Frobenius solutions> are therefore
$$
y_1(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^nz^n}{(2n)!},
\qquad
y_2(z)=z^{1/2}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^nz^n}{(2n+1)!}.
$$
Both analytic power-series factors converge everywhere by the <ratio test>. Recognizing the trigonometric series gives
$$
\boxed{y(z)=A\cos\sqrt z+B\sin\sqrt z.}
$$
On a punctured neighbourhood choose a consistent square-root branch for the second solution; the first is entire in $z$. These solutions are independent, since their leading orders are $1$ and $z^{1/2}$. A direct <square-root reduction of a regular-singular differential equation> also verifies the closed forms: for $s=\sqrt z$ and $Y(s)=y(s^2)$, $4zy''=Y_{ss}-Y_s/s$ and $2y'=Y_s/s$, so the equation reduces to $Y_{ss}+Y=0$.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

Write $u(x,y)=U(\xi,\eta)$. The <chain rule> gives $\partial_x=\partial_\xi+\partial_\eta$ and $\partial_y=2\partial_\xi+3\partial_\eta$. Consequently the coefficients of $U_{\xi\xi}$ and $U_{\eta\eta}$ cancel, while that of $U_{\xi\eta}$ is $12-25+12=-1$. The transformed <partial differential equation> is $-U_{\xi\eta}=1$. Integrating once in each variable gives the most general twice differentiable solution,
$$
\boxed{u(x,y)=-(x+2y)(x+3y)+F(x+2y)+G(x+3y),}
$$
where $F,G$ are arbitrary twice differentiable functions. The <change of variables> is invertible because its <Jacobian determinant> is $3-2=1$.

On $y=0$, the value condition gives $F(x)+G(x)=x^2$. Differentiating it yields $F'(x)+G'(x)=2x$. The derivative condition gives $-5x+2F'(x)+3G'(x)=x$. Subtraction therefore gives $G'(x)=2x$ and $F'(x)=0$. The constants in $F$ and $G$ cancel in their sum, leaving
$$
\boxed{u(x,y)=(x+3y)^2-(x+2y)(x+3y)=xy+3y^2.}
$$
Indeed $u_{xx}=0$, $u_{xy}=1$, $u_{yy}=6$, so the original operator gives $-5+6=1$, and the two initial-line conditions hold.

= 7B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= i
{parent=7b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the <first-order linear differential equation> by the <integrating factor> $e^{t/\alpha}$. Then
$$
\frac d{dt}(e^{t/\alpha}y)=\frac1\alpha e^{t/\alpha}f(t).
$$
If $y\to0$ in the remote past, then $e^{t/\alpha}y\to0$ there. Integration from $-\infty$ gives the necessary formula
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\frac1\alpha\int_{-\infty}^t e^{-(t-s)/\alpha}f(s)\,ds.}
$$
When this integral exists and tends to zero as $t\to-\infty$, differentiating verifies the equation and the past condition. Two such solutions differ by $Ce^{-t/\alpha}$, which diverges at $-\infty$ unless $C=0$, proving uniqueness. This is the <causal response of a first-order relaxation equation>, a <convolution> with its decaying <impulse response>.

The phrase “general function” implicitly requires compatibility with the past condition: it does not guarantee existence for arbitrary $f$. A sufficient condition is bounded forcing with $f(t)\to0$ as $t\to-\infty$; after setting $r=t-s$, the integral is bounded by the supremum of $|f|$ before $t$. For example, $f\equiv1$ gives $y=1+Ce^{-t/\alpha}$, so no solution has the stated past limit. Both forcings below vanish in the past and meet the intended causal condition, the impulse being interpreted distributionally.

= a
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Inserting the <Dirac delta> forcing into the integral gives the <impulse response>
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\frac1\alpha e^{-t/\alpha}H(t).}
$$
It is zero before the impulse and obeys the homogeneous equation afterward. Integrating the equation across zero gives $\alpha[y(0^+)-y(0^-)]=1$, exactly the jump $1/\alpha$ in this formula. Thus the formula also verifies the <differential equation> as a <distribution>; assigning a particular value at the jump does not affect that distribution.

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the <Heaviside step function> forcing, the integral begins at zero when $t>0$:
$$
y(t)=\frac1\alpha\int_0^t e^{-(t-s)/\alpha}\,ds=1-e^{-t/\alpha}.
$$
Before zero it vanishes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(t)=(1-e^{-t/\alpha})H(t).}
$$
This <causal response of a first-order relaxation equation> is continuous at zero and approaches the new constant equilibrium exponentially.

= ii
{parent=7b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The forcing is a unit pulse on $0<t<1$. For $t<0$, the homogeneous equation and continuity with $y(0)=0$ give $y=0$. On $0<t<1$, integration with $y(0)=0$ gives $y=1-e^{-t}$. At the end of the pulse its value is $1-e^{-1}$, and the subsequent homogeneous decay must start at that value. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\begin{cases}
0,&t\leq0,\\
1-e^{-t},&0\leq t\leq1,\\
(1-e^{-1})e^{-(t-1)},&t\geq1.
\end{cases}}
$$
Equivalently it is the difference of two translated step responses, $(1-e^{-t})H(t)-(1-e^{-(t-1)})H(t-1)$. The solution is continuous, rises concavely to its maximum $1-e^{-1}$ at $t=1$, and then decays exponentially to zero. Its derivative has jumps at the pulse edges; those are permitted because the right-hand side has step discontinuities.

\Image[paper-2-pulse-response.png]
{title=Continuous response to a unit-duration forcing pulse, rising to one minus exp(-1) and then decaying}

= 8B
{parent=Paper 2}
